{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting","id":1362822,"title":"What level of strategy do you consider is good for fighting ?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-01-23T01:35:03-08:00","id":1362822,"title":"What level of strategy do you consider is good for fighting ?","content":"So a while ago I discussed with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar531_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"531\" data-vbnamecard=\"531\">JohnDoe244</a> about some stuff where I had 2 different battle groups size 4 (but with the same stats) trying to overcome ennemies by a strategical attack on 2 different spots, where these spots were close enough that they were in the same scene, but far enough that one character cannot act on them at the same time (Surely long range, maybe Medium), and he basically answered (iirc) : &quot;You should have put them together as a Battle Group size 5&quot;. <br /> <br /> That's when I started questioning if Exalted actually wanted its players (ST included) to &quot;have strategic ideas&quot;, and now I'm thinking it rather wants the players to describe strategic ideas and confront them through the system : It is because you rolled a good strategy War roll that you can describe the ennemies as flanked (because you took the stratagem), not the other way around. And then during fight, especially with the Battle Groups, I don't think you are supposed to play strategy through simple DD tactics (&quot;They are close to the sea and we are blocking them, so they get the Maneuver Back to the sea !&quot;), but rather through description and mechanics confrontation (&quot;The fight has already started and you want to add a new maneuver because it has been 4 turns already ? Yeah it seems time to shake things up, make a Strategy War roll against opposing general and we'll see what is the new fight disposition&quot;)<br /> <br /> This first one came rather obvious when I realized it, because the strategy are explicit in the books. What I'm starting to wonder is, is the rest of the fighting system supposed to be played like that as well ? I know I have discussed lengthy debated with people because we for example disagreed on should the players continue to withering past 15 initiative because it is an optimal play, but it might also be detrimental to the flow and tension of the game. Should you make a decisive attack because you then expose yourself to an Initiative reversal (I think that's the name ?) is something <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1121_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1121\" data-vbnamecard=\"1121\">DrLoveMonkey</a> usually reminds people when considering fighting strategy, but I'm starting to wonder if the game rather wants you to play from a &quot;strategic point of view&quot; (you chose a course of action because it assures you, the player, a win for your character) rather than from a &quot;narrative point of view&quot; (you play what is more dramatic in the moment, and then let the system takes its course, which might result in a loss for your character). I think you can some sort of see it the way &quot;Card counting&quot; is considered in Gambling games : While not strictly forbidden, it is usually discouraged, for money reasons of course, but as well because it changes the bluff and social dynamic of the game (you wouldn't do this with your fellow friends with no money at stake because at this point you might as well be doing Calculus College courses and it's not something you want to do at 10 pm on a Friday)<br /> <br /> So my final question is : What level of strategy do you practice, and expect from other players and the ST, while in the game about the following topics : <br /> <br /> - Battle groups (Positioning, dividing, strategic, etc ...) I'm talking about dividing here because usually players wants fo example to consider a different battle group between their infantry and their archers, but technically these characters have the same stats (they are basic soldiers), just maybe a different weapon, so while I don't think they want to explicitly abuse the game by asking for this, I think it's not in the purpose of the Battle Groups to make this kind of separation.<br /> <br /> - Direct Fighting System (Initiative accumulation and launching decisives, motes management, Gambit use, etc ...) I think this is the topic most people encounter in game and the one that generates the most frustration when it does not go well, because it is usually the most iconic of the genre. <br /> <br /> - Social System (Intimacy management, Intimacy guessing). I think this one is actually not that easy for players and ST alike. Because in fighting the system is very clear on wether someone has a bonus or a specific mechanic or not. In intimacies it's totally not that clear : Sometimes you make a description about a character or their acting and the player do totally not understand what you meant by that, or are lead to believe something different to what you want to communicate, and the only way to fix this is to meta-tell the players &quot;Your character understands it means this&quot;, because there was a lack of communication. At first it appeared clear to me that players have to get what you are saying, but with experience I understood that it made no sense to me to let the character do mistakes on this topic because you had miscommunication with the player.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T03:45:54-08:00","id":1362836,"title":"","content":"I don't remember the context of the previous discussion, so I'll answer generally.<br /> <br /> Exalted is a narrative game, not a tactical one.<br /> <br /> Tactics are just a way of getting a Stunt bonus.<br /> <br /> As for the specific battlegroup example, you use whatever number of battle groups makes sense. In your above examples, I'd use 1.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T04:09:35-08:00","id":1362839,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting#post1362822\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Battle groups (Positioning, dividing, strategic, etc ...) I'm talking about dividing here because usually players wants fo example to consider a different battle group between their infantry and their archers, but technically these characters have the same stats (they are basic soldiers), just maybe a different weapon, so while I don't think they want to explicitly abuse the game by asking for this, I think it's not in the purpose of the Battle Groups to make this kind of separation.</div> </div> </div> </div>The most important part for me as a Storyteller is a level of narrative seperation between the two battle groups so there's appropriate consequences for one of them getting wiped out.<br /> <br /> To go with your example, the infantry and the archers might have the same statblock as QCs but in peace time they're probably from different places in the society of whatever hypothetical country raised this army that could be a factor if they don't come home. If story-wise they're interchangable goons equally skilled in the spear, sword and bow it's less likely to be worth drawing a distinction.<br /> <br /> As a final note, I'm also willing to run mixed units if there's differences in physical ability but not in immediate goals or narrative role i.e. my players recently summoned some elementals and demons to protect an area and I told them that for the purpose of this combat I'd roll them all in together rather than have 5 demons and 5 elementals as different participants.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T04:28:02-08:00","id":1362845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362839#post1362839\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The most important part for me as a Storyteller is a level of narrative seperation between the two battle groups so there's appropriate consequences for one of them getting wiped out.<br /> <br /> To go with your example, the infantry and the archers might have the same statblock as QCs but in peace time they're probably from different places in the society of whatever hypothetical country raised this army that could be a factor if they don't come home. If story-wise they're interchangable goons equally skilled in the spear, sword and bow it's less likely to be worth drawing a distinction.<br /> <br /> As a final note, I'm also willing to run mixed units if there's differences in physical ability but not in immediate goals or narrative role i.e. my players recently summoned some elementals and demons to protect an area and I told them that for the purpose of this combat I'd roll them all in together rather than have 5 demons and 5 elementals as different participants.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah I think mixing summoned units of sorcery lvl 1 into a battle group of Elite soldiers with some special merits or something like this is probably my next step of &quot;Handling summoned units&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T05:38:13-08:00","id":1362857,"title":"","content":"I think, for most of the rules in the game, it's totally fine to think strategically about them. This is because their strategic and narrative nature are not in conflict.<br /> <br /> For instance, your character does not know the phrase &quot;initiative shift&quot;, but they know that going for a killing blow can put you in a vulnerable position, and (if they have any rating in a combat ability), they know tactics to mitigate that. Thus you can generally assume that the tactic you choose with your knowledge of the combat system matches up with the tactic they choose with their knowledge of combat.<br /> <br /> With various charms that offer tradeoffs between different resources or various dice tricks, these are all supposed to be part of the character's general skill, so one can generally assume that part of that skill is combining them in whatever the player thinks is the optimal way. In particular, there is no narrative reason to avoid doing this, because there is no clear narrative meaning, say to using Dipping Swallow Defense and not an excellency, or vice versa, to parrying an attack. Even for charms that do have a clear narrative meaning, generally speaking it fits the narrative to use them in a strategic way, because Exalted isn't supposed to be a story about people with various amazing powers who use them to no real effect.<br /> <br /> However, there are certain areas of the game where the rules as seen by the players are more different from the dynamics as seen by the characters, and using them in a maximally strategic way would be harmful. The most extreme of this is battlegroups, where by far the most powerful strategy is to divide them up into multiple battlegroups. The battle group rules seem designed in part to simulate the narrative trope where a group of similar foes is less effective than a single individual foe (the inverse ninja principle). This is not supposed to be something that the characters are aware of, and since it's something that makes the combat more complicated without being more fun, the Storyteller is supposed to militate against splitting up battlegroups.<br /> <br /> Another example might be Limit Break, which is also something the characters are not supposed to be aware of and narratively not something they can prepare for or mitigate, but that probably comes up less.<br /> <br /> But I think most rules, it's fine to think strategically about.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure about the social influence system as I haven't used it much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25834-habitableexoplanet","name":"habitableexoplanet","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"296"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T05:55:41-08:00","id":1362860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>habitableexoplanet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362857#post1362857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think, for most of the rules in the game, it's totally fine to think strategically about them. This is because their strategic and narrative nature are not in conflict.<br /> <br /> For instance, your character does not know the phrase &quot;initiative shift&quot;, but they know that going for a killing blow can put you in a vulnerable position, and (if they have any rating in a combat ability), they know tactics to mitigate that.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm really unsure about this first part. The Exalted core clearly states : <span style=\"color:#000000\"> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">To be clear, </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">withering </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">and </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">decisive </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">attacks are a game </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">abstraction used to model the cinematic nature of combat between heroes in Creation. Individuals within the world </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">of </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Exalted </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">would recognize no such distinction; setting aside feints and the like, characters absolutely intend for most </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">withering </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">attacks to injure or kill their target. The players and Storyteller declare attacks as </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">withering </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">or </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">decisive </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">to create satisfying and exciting battles; for char-</span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">acters, each cut and thrust is made in deadly earnest.</span><br /> <br /> <br /> </span><br /> I think what is complicated is that the player chose to make a withering attack &quot;before the action&quot;, but what makes it a withering inside the game (ie. an attack that glances) is only shown after the roll and the overall action. <br /> <br /> Though, by rereading the section, I believe it is clear the existence of the initiative mini-game is indeed to make the player try and act strategically to at least some extent about the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T06:20:59-08:00","id":1362866,"title":"","content":"The initiative combat engine is definitely supposed to be played tactically. It is, or at least is supposed to, play like a lightsaber duel, where both sides clash but every strike and sweep either gets blocked or dodged until somebody gets the other on the ropes and they just can\u2019t protect themselves anymore. <br /> <br /> On the other hand Exalted has a lot more aspects than, say, DnD where it goes \u201cyeah technically you can do X, but really you shouldn\u2019t... for example nothing is stopping a character from taking 6 tyrant lizard familiars right off chargen, but that would be a nightmare in combat and especially if it was a DB game WILDLY unbalanced in any area they could possibly be used. It\u2019s not really appropriate to do that kind of thing most of the time.<br /> <br /> As for your example I\u2019d keep the battlegroups split if the point of the battle is that they\u2019re far enough away from eachother that the general is trying to force the enemy to split up to fight it. At that point the whole crux of the combat scene is that there\u2019s an enemy at both gates and the players need to divide their strength, or be fast, or use some other clever tactic to get an edge. There\u2019s other things that I would probably simply allow as well. Like an army that\u2019s attacking a fortress or previously entrenched position, the defenders will have fortifications set up already that give some of them cover, regardless of the strategies employed. Then you can add stratagems on top of that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T06:32:56-08:00","id":1362869,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362866#post1362866\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The initiative combat engine is definitely supposed to be played tactically. It is, or at least is supposed to, play like a lightsaber duel, where both sides clash but every strike and sweep either gets blocked or dodged until somebody gets the other on the ropes and they just can\u2019t protect themselves anymore.<br /> <br /> On the other hand Exalted has a lot more aspects than, say, DnD where it goes \u201cyeah technically you can do X, but really you shouldn\u2019t... for example nothing is stopping a character from taking 6 tyrant lizard familiars right off chargen, but that would be a nightmare in combat and especially if it was a DB game WILDLY unbalanced in any area they could possibly be used. It\u2019s not really appropriate to do that kind of thing most of the time.<br /> <br /> As for your example I\u2019d keep the battlegroups split if the point of the battle is that they\u2019re far enough away from eachother that the general is trying to force the enemy to split up to fight it. At that point the whole crux of the combat scene is that there\u2019s an enemy at both gates and the players need to divide their strength, or be fast, or use some other clever tactic to get an edge. There\u2019s other things that I would probably simply allow as well. Like an army that\u2019s attacking a fortress or previously entrenched position, the defenders will have fortifications set up already that give some of them cover, regardless of the strategies employed. Then you can add stratagems on top of that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agree on the first 2 paragraphs.<br /> <br /> I think if the 2 gates are in the same &quot;scene&quot;, the Battle group should be considered one battle group, and the enemy forces taking a point should be incarnated via a lieutenant or something like that whose objective is to take this specific point. But I know that before discussing this topic here, I would consider dividing the battle group in 2 and it would not be an efficacious use of the rules.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T07:33:23-08:00","id":1362885,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362869#post1362869\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Agree on the first 2 paragraphs.<br /> <br /> I think if the 2 gates are in the same &amp;quot;scene&amp;quot;, the Battle group should be considered one battle group, and the enemy forces taking a point should be incarnated via a lieutenant or something like that whose objective is to take this specific point. But I know that before discussing this topic here, I would consider dividing the battle group in 2 and it would not be an efficacious use of the rules.</div> </div> </div> </div>It might depend on exact positioning. If the two gates are beside eachother or something then I agree, but if they\u2019re on opposing sides of a city or something like that they should be separate enough to count as two. For one you\u2019d have command actions issued on one side of the city affect soldiers way on the other side without any friendly connections between the two. Same with splitting up archers cavalry and infantry. Archers can attack up to long range and can\u2019t exclude allies from their assault, even if they use the same stats that\u2019s different enough to split them off. <br /> <br /> Armies are supposed to occasionally consist of multiple battlegroups, that\u2019s why one of the penalties for a rout check is \u201canother allied battlegroup has already suffered dissolution during the fight.\u201d","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T07:58:40-08:00","id":1362888,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362885#post1362885\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It might depend on exact positioning. If the two gates are beside eachother or something then I agree, but if they\u2019re on opposing sides of a city or something like that they should be separate enough to count as two. For one you\u2019d have command actions issued on one side of the city affect soldiers way on the other side without any friendly connections between the two. Same with splitting up archers cavalry and infantry. Archers can attack up to long range and can\u2019t exclude allies from their assault, even if they use the same stats that\u2019s different enough to split them off.<br /> <br /> Armies are supposed to occasionally consist of multiple battlegroups, that\u2019s why one of the penalties for a rout check is \u201canother allied battlegroup has already suffered dissolution during the fight.\u201d</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree mainly and I suspect we don't understand each other when I say &quot;In the same scene&quot;, which means like in the same battle scene, in the same place. Two opposed gates in a city that are further in range than extreme would most likely not be in the same scene.<br /> <br /> I'm actually not sure about the archers/soldiers part. As Lioness said, when the unit is really different from a plot perspective (&quot;The archers of Alken have come in renfort, it is said they can shoot birds up to 200 feet !&quot;) I'm pretty sure I will do a distinction, but if the strategy is &quot;I have infantry on the front and archers on the back&quot;, well I think this is a waste of time and energy to manage a Battle Group for archers because they feel different in gameplay, but would otherwise tell the fight in the same manner, I think this should rather be used as a Stunt bonus, or War Tactic modifier, like JohnDoe22 suggested.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T08:14:35-08:00","id":1362892,"title":"","content":"They might tell the fight in a different manner though, for instance if they\u2019ve got a line of infantry in the front and are arcing arrows over the infantry to hit your back line that might give one of the super-mobile characters an opportunity to leap past the front line and engage the archers who are pincushioning your troops. Especially if fortifications are involved; you might be engaging a group of spear soldiers at the gate while the archers on top of the walls are in heavy cover (+2 defense).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T08:33:00-08:00","id":1362897,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362892#post1362892\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They might tell the fight in a different manner though, for instance if they\u2019ve got a line of infantry in the front and are arcing arrows over the infantry to hit your back line that might give one of the super-mobile characters an opportunity to leap past the front line and engage the archers who are pincushioning your troops. Especially if fortifications are involved; you might be engaging a group of spear soldiers at the gate while the archers on top of the walls are in heavy cover (+2 defense).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah but I think this is really where you need to put an important general archer on the backline to put the emphase on the super mobile character, and not just divide your Battle Group in 2 groups. <br /> <br /> Wouldn't the Fortification stratagem better represent your second option, instead of resorting to dividing the Battle Group ? The defending soldiers would first fire against the opposing army from behind the wall, benefitting the heavy cover, and when the attacking army is close enough they would switch to swords and the like. It doesn't mean there are no archer anymore, but rather that once the troops have made contact, there is no narrative need to make a separate battle group for the archers and the infantry, the Battle Group attack roll represent them fighting the attacking forces that have made contact in melee and at range as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T08:53:08-08:00","id":1362900,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362860#post1362860\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm really unsure about this first part. The Exalted core clearly states : <span style=\"color:#000000\"> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">To be clear, </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">withering </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">and </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">decisive </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">attacks are a game </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">abstraction used to model the cinematic nature of combat between heroes in Creation. Individuals within the world </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">of </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Exalted </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">would recognize no such distinction; setting aside feints and the like, characters absolutely intend for most </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">withering </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">attacks to injure or kill their target. The players and Storyteller declare attacks as </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">withering </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">or </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">decisive </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">to create satisfying and exciting battles; for char-</span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">acters, each cut and thrust is made in deadly earnest.</span><br /> <br /> <br /> </span><br /> I think what is complicated is that the player chose to make a withering attack &quot;before the action&quot;, but what makes it a withering inside the game (ie. an attack that glances) is only shown after the roll and the overall action.<br /> <br /> Though, by rereading the section, I believe it is clear the existence of the initiative mini-game is indeed to make the player try and act strategically to at least some extent about the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for pointing out that line, I didn't see it or didn't remember it. It certainly runs counter to what I was saying. Still I think there's no real conflict as long as one doesn't interpret it completely dogmatically. For instance I would say that both in the real world, and in the kind of stories Exalted is based on, attacks lie on a continuum of how much the fighters commitment to them. In a real fight this is based on things like weight distribution and footwork, and in the more fantastical stories things like how dramatically you shout the name of the attack before doing it. Generally attacks with more commitment are more likely to hit and do real damage, but put you in a more vulnerable position if you miss, or if you hit and someone else attacks you. When you're doing an attack with less commitment, you certainly intend for it to hit, but you're also preparing for the fact that your opponent will almost certainly find a way to block it, and thinking of your next move. So withering attacks model one end of the spectrum and decisive attacks model the other, where for attacks in the middle this could be decided only after the attacks happen.<br /> <br /> When I narrate my attacks in game, I tend to narrate withering attacks as things like swiping my opponent's weapon out of the way, striking their arm, knocking them off balance, etc. rather than describing a lethal blow and having the Storyteller describe how they slip away. But that's just my personal preference.<br /> <br /> However I would certainly say there are some attacks which the characters must essentially understand to be withering or decisive. I'm thinking of charms that create a unique physical manifestation that are also withering-only or decisive-only. For a random example from the core book, you can take Fakhuru's Seven Venoms Exhalation. I think he knows that this attack never actually kills anyone (or, if you rule that it does kill people in battlegroups, only rarely kills people) but knocks his opponents down, weakens them, knocks them off balance, etc. and that his bites and claws are comparatively more lethal (even if he doesn't know that he's using Claw-Fang Maelstrom).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25834-habitableexoplanet","name":"habitableexoplanet","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"296"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T09:11:51-08:00","id":1362902,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>habitableexoplanet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362900#post1362900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> However I would certainly say there are some attacks which the characters must essentially understand to be withering or decisive. I'm thinking of charms that create a unique physical manifestation that are also withering-only or decisive-only. For a random example from the core book, you can take Fakhuru's Seven Venoms Exhalation. I think he knows that this attack never actually kills anyone (or, if you rule that it does kill people in battlegroups, only rarely kills people) but knocks his opponents down, weakens them, knocks them off balance, etc. and that his bites and claws are comparatively more lethal (even if he doesn't know that he's using Claw-Fang Maelstrom).</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yeah I never thought about Charms that allows for Withering or Decisive attack. I don't know if it means that the attacker know it is not lethal, but it sure makes things harder to understand clearly. I think at this point the design of the Charm is more designed to talk to the player making strategic choices &quot;Ok here I wish to do a withering attack so I will use the withering Charm&quot; than the character deciding to use a non-lethal technique.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T09:15:20-08:00","id":1362903,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362897#post1362897\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah but I think this is really where you need to put an important general archer on the backline to put the emphase on the super mobile character, and not just divide your Battle Group in 2 groups.</div> </div> </div> </div>One or the other or both even works. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362897#post1362897\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wouldn't the Fortification stratagem better represent your second option, instead of resorting to dividing the Battle Group ? The defending soldiers would first fire against the opposing army from behind the wall, benefitting the heavy cover, and when the attacking army is close enough they would switch to swords and the like. It doesn't mean there are no archer anymore, but rather that once the troops have made contact, there is no narrative need to make a separate battle group for the archers and the infantry, the Battle Group attack roll represent them fighting the attacking forces that have made contact in melee and at range as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>The fortifications stratagem isn\u2019t quite like that. Technically it provides no cover bonus to anyone, the enemy is just at long range through difficult terrain because the defending army has set up massive tangles of stakes and pitfalls and stuff.<br /> <br /> I think, I\u2019m not 100% sure, but I think that stratagems are meant to be used in tandem with other battlefield considerations.<br /> <br /> As for the narrative difference between them, there might not really be one but there is a tactical difference. If they\u2019re one battlegroup half of them can attack enemy archers and the other half can\u2019t, half has +2 defense and can\u2019t parry, the other doesn\u2019t. Just like how there might not be a narrative distinction between a PC archer fighting from Close or Short range, they\u2019re both still just fighting, we make a tactical distinction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T10:13:05-08:00","id":1362910,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362903#post1362903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The fortifications stratagem isn\u2019t quite like that. Technically it provides no cover bonus to anyone, the enemy is just at long range through difficult terrain because the defending army has set up massive tangles of stakes and pitfalls and stuff.<br /> <br /> I think, I\u2019m not 100% sure, but I think that stratagems are meant to be used in tandem with other battlefield considerations.<br /> <br /> As for the narrative difference between them, there might not really be one but there is a tactical difference. If they\u2019re one battlegroup half of them can attack enemy archers and the other half can\u2019t, half has +2 defense and can\u2019t parry, the other doesn\u2019t. Just like how there might not be a narrative distinction between a PC archer fighting from Close or Short range, they\u2019re both still just fighting, we make a tactical distinction.</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't really wanted to keep the Cover bonus, but since you had an idea I wanted to integrate it into my solution. By the rules, the Fortification Stratagem makes what you said, but I think it's acceptable to invent another Stratagem that works with more of a cover bonus instead of something else, or just with a higher cap success. <br /> <br /> My idea is that it's really the other way around when you say &quot;I think, I\u2019m not 100% sure, but I think that stratagems are meant to be used in tandem with other battlefield considerations.&quot;. I think the battlefields consideration should be taken into account before the stratagem war roll to define bonuses to the roll of the general, and then you find yourself in the situation given by the result of this roll. <br /> <br /> For your last paragraph, yes that's how I thought at first, but I'm trying to think about how the battle groups should not be handled in a strategic manner, from the mechanic of the game viewpoint, because they are not here to allow for strategic gameplay but rather the opposite, they are here to simplify the &quot;lots of people&quot; gameplay and let the heroes shine instead. I'm not arguing like I'm 100% right here, I'm more trying to see &quot;What if I decided to play like this ?&quot;, and I think there is a value to consider the things this way. I guess you have as well handled battle groups in a big fight, where there are archers and infantry and cavalerie and stuff and in the end you spend your managing boring group of NPCs (they are not boring on their own but when you have more of them it kinda becomes) because someone said &quot;We should have infantry here and archers behind them&quot;, which is a fine tactic for description and narration, but I don't think it goes all the way the system actually want you to play the Battle Groups.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T11:52:25-08:00","id":1362923,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362910#post1362910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My idea is that it's really the other way around when you say &amp;quot;I think, I\u2019m not 100% sure, but I think that stratagems are meant to be used in tandem with other battlefield considerations.&amp;quot;. I think the battlefields consideration should be taken into account before the stratagem war roll to define bonuses to the roll of the general, and then you find yourself in the situation given by the result of this roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing I don\u2019t like about that is you could spend a bunch of sessions and scenes building advantages, like being entrenched in a fortress city, making deals with local spirits, setting up undermines to collapse under the enemy, and then when the battle finally comes you only get to pick one stratagem, and if you lose the roll then all of a sudden your walls disappear and the elementals go away and your mines can\u2019t be used.<br /> <br /> You would get a bonus to the roll, but a bit of bad luck, or facing somebody with a higher dice pool it just vanishes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362910#post1362910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For your last paragraph, yes that's how I thought at first, but I'm trying to think about how the battle groups should not be handled in a strategic manner, from the mechanic of the game viewpoint, because they are not here to allow for strategic gameplay but rather the opposite, they are here to simplify the &amp;quot;lots of people&amp;quot; gameplay and let the heroes shine instead. I'm not arguing like I'm 100% right here, I'm more trying to see &amp;quot;What if I decided to play like this ?&amp;quot;, and I think there is a value to consider the things this way. I guess you have as well handled battle groups in a big fight, where there are archers and infantry and cavalerie and stuff and in the end you spend your managing boring group of NPCs (they are not boring on their own but when you have more of them it kinda becomes) because someone said &amp;quot;We should have infantry here and archers behind them&amp;quot;, which is a fine tactic for description and narration, but I don't think it goes all the way the system actually want you to play the Battle Groups.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I guess it depends how simple you want to make the scene. I\u2019ve had scenes before where there were battlegroups and I just asked my players to roll (Dex + melee) and if they get even a single success they just win the fight and we move on without even rolling join battle. One roll, almost impossible to fail, then quickly to the next thing. It\u2019s a good option for when there\u2019s nobody that can challenge the PCs and nothing interesting going on there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T14:38:54-08:00","id":1362957,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362902#post1362902\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh yeah I never thought about Charms that allows for Withering or Decisive attack. I don't know if it means that the attacker know it is not lethal, but it sure makes things harder to understand clearly. I think at this point the design of the Charm is more designed to talk to the player making strategic choices &quot;Ok here I wish to do a withering attack so I will use the withering Charm&quot; than the character deciding to use a non-lethal technique.</div> </div> </div> </div> I went through the core book and looked at all the withering-only attack charms outside of the antagonists section. I divided them into categories:<br /> <br /> [A] The attack has a specific effect on the opponent other than initiative damage - knocking them back/down, stunning them, knocking them out, or similar. In the fiction, it\u2019s most likely that the character is attempting to achieve this effect and not directly trying to kill. You could also narrate it as an attempted killing blow that fails and has a lesser effect, but this seems like a more niche approach.<br /> 4 charms (Force without Fire, Ox-Stunning Blow, Knockout Blow, Shockwave Technique).<br /> <br /> <br /> [B] The attack has no special rules. It's probably just a normal attack that happens to never kill anyone, though you could also flavor it as a technique the character knows to get into a better position.<br /> 9 charms (Titan-Straightening Method, Circle of Bright Reaving, Precision of the Striking Raptor, Savage Wolf Attack, Striking Fury Claws, Seven Points of Weakness Strike, Empowering Justice Redirection, Azure Abacus Meditation, Double Attack Technique, Triple Attack Technique, Dervish Blade Frenzy)<br /> Striking Fury Claws is pretty ridiculous as the text talks about disemboweling people but it never does decisive damage. Guess the bowels aren\u2019t that important\u2026<br /> Azure Abacus Meditation also has a clear visual effect, which it\u2019s weird thinking about the martial artist realizing that they never kill anyone when they get that. On the other hand some of them like Precision of the Striking Raptor are clearly not a separate technique the Solar is aware of but just a general increase in skill.<br /> <br /> [C] The attack is a withering attack but does decisive damage. In the fiction, this is surely an attempt to kill the opponent and stop their attack or achieve another goal simultaneously:<br /> 2 charms (Heavens Crash Down, Flashing Edge of Dawn).<br /> <br /> <br /> [D] I can see arguments for both sides:<br /> Revolving Bow Discipline - maybe it's a series of arrow shots intended to make the target fight defensively (in rules terms, crash), like modern-day suppressive fire.<br /> Shower of Deadly Blades - in the mechanics is just a normal withering attack, but text says \u201cnormally used to scatter groups of enemies\u201d so you could say the character knows this technique is about calling fear and disarray rather than death.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25834-habitableexoplanet","name":"habitableexoplanet","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"296"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T14:42:50-08:00","id":1362959,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362903#post1362903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One or the other or both even works.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The fortifications stratagem isn\u2019t quite like that. Technically it provides no cover bonus to anyone, the enemy is just at long range through difficult terrain because the defending army has set up massive tangles of stakes and pitfalls and stuff.<br /> <br /> I think, I\u2019m not 100% sure, but I think that stratagems are meant to be used in tandem with other battlefield considerations.<br /> <br /> As for the narrative difference between them, there might not really be one but there is a tactical difference. If they\u2019re one battlegroup half of them can attack enemy archers and the other half can\u2019t, half has +2 defense and can\u2019t parry, the other doesn\u2019t. Just like how there might not be a narrative distinction between a PC archer fighting from Close or Short range, they\u2019re both still just fighting, we make a tactical distinction.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The way I would describe it is that stratagems are intended for, if the players and storyteller have no advance plans for what the battlefield is going to be, giving the Storyteller a starting point, but if the battle is already decided, to add on to what's already there without messing up the previous story to much. So it's probably supposed to be a little bit flexible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25834-habitableexoplanet","name":"habitableexoplanet","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"296"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T15:11:19-08:00","id":1362964,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>habitableexoplanet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362957#post1362957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I went through the core book and looked at all the withering-only attack charms outside of the antagonists section. I divided them into categories:<br /> <br /> [A] The attack has a specific effect on the opponent other than initiative damage - knocking them back/down, stunning them, knocking them out, or similar. In the fiction, it\u2019s most likely that the character is attempting to achieve this effect and not directly trying to kill. You could also narrate it as an attempted killing blow that fails and has a lesser effect, but this seems like a more niche approach.<br /> 4 charms (Force without Fire, Ox-Stunning Blow, Knockout Blow, Shockwave Technique).<br /> <br /> <br /> [B] The attack has no special rules. It's probably just a normal attack that happens to never kill anyone, though you could also flavor it as a technique the character knows to get into a better position.<br /> 9 charms (Titan-Straightening Method, Circle of Bright Reaving, Precision of the Striking Raptor, Savage Wolf Attack, Striking Fury Claws, Seven Points of Weakness Strike, Empowering Justice Redirection, Azure Abacus Meditation, Double Attack Technique, Triple Attack Technique, Dervish Blade Frenzy)<br /> Striking Fury Claws is pretty ridiculous as the text talks about disemboweling people but it never does decisive damage. Guess the bowels aren\u2019t that important\u2026<br /> Azure Abacus Meditation also has a clear visual effect, which it\u2019s weird thinking about the martial artist realizing that they never kill anyone when they get that. On the other hand some of them like Precision of the Striking Raptor are clearly not a separate technique the Solar is aware of but just a general increase in skill.<br /> <br /> [C] The attack is a withering attack but does decisive damage. In the fiction, this is surely an attempt to kill the opponent and stop their attack or achieve another goal simultaneously:<br /> 2 charms (Heavens Crash Down, Flashing Edge of Dawn).<br /> <br /> <br /> [D] I can see arguments for both sides:<br /> Revolving Bow Discipline - maybe it's a series of arrow shots intended to make the target fight defensively (in rules terms, crash), like modern-day suppressive fire.<br /> Shower of Deadly Blades - in the mechanics is just a normal withering attack, but text says \u201cnormally used to scatter groups of enemies\u201d so you could say the character knows this technique is about calling fear and disarray rather than death.</div> </div> </div> </div>Keep in mind that Withering damage can kill battlegroupies, so things get even more confusing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25764-vwllss-trnt-prncss","name":"vwllss trnt prncss","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"244"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T17:56:15-08:00","id":1362980,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vwllss trnt prncss</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362964#post1362964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Keep in mind that Withering damage can kill battlegroupies, so things get even more confusing.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Watch how you're talking, pal. I could disembowel you with my bare hands - at least if you had a couple of your friends standing next to you.&quot;<br /> <br /> I think you can also do lethal decisive damage to a battlegroup and defeat it without killing anyone, just scaring them off. Battlegroups are the Wild West of fiction-rules correspondence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25834-habitableexoplanet","name":"habitableexoplanet","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"296"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-26T04:33:49-08:00","id":1363432,"title":"","content":"I must admit that I always kind of ignore this part about withering and decisive being invisible to the characters, because to me, it makes perfect sense in the fiction.<br /> <br /> As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1121_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1121\" data-vbnamecard=\"1121\">DrLoveMonkey</a> said withering attacks are clearly the &quot;wiff&quot; part of the lightsaber duel. The point is, that even though all these strikes would cause harm if not parried, it is clear to the combatant that they will be parried and thus are not intended as genuine attacks. At some point you see the opening created by your attacks and you now strike with the actual hope to hit, possibly leaving an opening yourself.<br /> <br /> This kind of breaks down with ranged attacks, but then I'm not entirely sure that withering attacks model fiction well in their case and I think it would be an interesting direction to treat them similarly to combat sorcery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","name":"Lanic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13416_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"194"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-26T05:46:15-08:00","id":1363439,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1362923#post1362923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The thing I don\u2019t like about that is you could spend a bunch of sessions and scenes building advantages, like being entrenched in a fortress city, making deals with local spirits, setting up undermines to collapse under the enemy, and then when the battle finally comes you only get to pick one stratagem, and if you lose the roll then all of a sudden your walls disappear and the elementals go away and your mines can\u2019t be used.<br /> <br /> You would get a bonus to the roll, but a bit of bad luck, or facing somebody with a higher dice pool it just vanishes.</div> </div> </div> </div>My bad I just forgot to answer on this topic I'll catch on. I must admit this is a flaw I haven't been able to address. Maybe by granting new units (Battle groups and lieutenants) to the side amassing advantages ?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-26T14:24:54-08:00","id":1363514,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lanic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1363432#post1363432\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I must admit that I always kind of ignore this part about withering and decisive being invisible to the characters, because to me, it makes perfect sense in the fiction.<br /> <br /> As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1121_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1121\" data-vbnamecard=\"1121\">DrLoveMonkey</a> said withering attacks are clearly the &quot;wiff&quot; part of the lightsaber duel. The point is, that even though all these strikes would cause harm if not parried, it is clear to the combatant that they will be parried and thus are not intended as genuine attacks. At some point you see the opening created by your attacks and you now strike with the actual hope to hit, possibly leaving an opening yourself.<br /> <br /> This kind of breaks down with ranged attacks, but then I'm not entirely sure that withering attacks model fiction well in their case and I think it would be an interesting direction to treat them similarly to combat sorcery.</div> </div> </div> </div>As a fighting game player, Withering ranged attacks make perfect sense to me. A good 90% of the time I'm throwing a fireball, I don't think it has any chance of a clean hit. But I'm not throwing that fireball for damage, I'm throwing it to reduce my opponent's options. They might jump it (now I know their movement path for a little while), block it (now they're in blockstun for a little while), counter-fireball (now they're in recovery for a little while) etc etc. By forcing them to deal with my fireball, I can exploit a temporary information and mobility advantage caused by their dealing with my fireball. <br /> And heck, there's the Street Fighter classic of &quot;Hadoken, enemy jumps over it, Shoryuken while they can't dodge&quot;. That's a <i>perfect</i> example of a Withering ranged attack followed by a Decisive attack. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1363439#post1363439\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> My bad I just forgot to answer on this topic I'll catch on. I must admit this is a flaw I haven't been able to address. Maybe by granting new units (Battle groups and lieutenants) to the side amassing advantages ?</div> </div> </div> </div>I always saw the strategy roll as being like a last-moment Introduce Fact / Challenge Fact. &quot;Because I'm a military genius, I sent half my forces LEFT and your scouts didn't notice! Haha!&quot; or &quot;Because I'm a great general, he couldn't have flanked me! There's a marsh he didn't know about there so his flankers are going to arrive late!&quot; <br /> Once an element of battle is established, such as the walls, demons, mines, etc, it's just <i>there</i> unless somebody pops a strategem to work around it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25764-vwllss-trnt-prncss","name":"vwllss trnt prncss","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"244"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-26T15:24:11-08:00","id":1363519,"title":"","content":"I feel like players of Exalted will have a lot more fun if they, knowing that they will get into a big battle, think about what in the game setting might help them win the battle, and what they can do to take advantage of it. I think the Storyteller will also have fun if the players engage with the world in this way. So of course the Storyteller should do everything possible to reward the players for this. This absolutely can include just saying &quot;You gain the benefits of ____ Stratagem&quot; or even bigger benefits. (If the players are in a fortified town, they should at least gain the benefits of heavy cover for everyone on the walls. If the players start recruiting the town's craftspeople to build additional fortifications outside the walls, or recruiting local water and earth spirits to make a temporary moat, or whatever, they should absolutely get additional fabulous benefits on top of that.) I don't think the Storyteller should let anything in the rulebook dissuade them from doing that, and I also don't think the strategic combat system is intended to rule out this stuff.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, I don't think it would be very fun, in a game like Exalted, to reason out the moment-to-moment position of their characters and how it affects the strategy. I don't think Storytellers should reward the players for drawing a diagram of their character's movement and the enemy's movement, and explaining where they go next and how it benefits them, so I'm happy that the game abstracts that stuff away. I'm also fine with games like 3rd and 4th edition D&amp;D that expect some tactical movement. <br /> <br /> The &quot;splitting up battlegroups&quot; think is tricky because its mechanical benefits are so out of proportion to its narrative meaning. I think the best reason to split up battlegroups, or merge them, is probably to equalize spotlight time and effectiveness between different players. If a sorcerer character is not very effective in a big combat (unlikely but possible I guess), why not make their summoned beasties their own battlegroup, and let them roll that battlegroup's attacks? If someone has a social character who is feeling left out in a siege, why not let them inspire a specific subgroup of allies to take a special role in combat as a split-off battlegroup. On the other hand, if the War supernal is the player who feels most responsible for the soldiers and the archers, and also the player who's going to be having the most fun in a big strategic combat, maybe find a way to make the soldiers and archers one big battlegroup.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25834-habitableexoplanet","name":"habitableexoplanet","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"296"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-26T23:52:52-08:00","id":1363566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vwllss trnt prncss</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1363514#post1363514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always saw the strategy roll as being like a last-moment Introduce Fact / Challenge Fact. &quot;Because I'm a military genius, I sent half my forces LEFT and your scouts didn't notice! Haha!&quot; or &quot;Because I'm a great general, he couldn't have flanked me! There's a marsh he didn't know about there so his flankers are going to arrive late!&quot;<br /> Once an element of battle is established, such as the walls, demons, mines, etc, it's just <i>there</i> unless somebody pops a strategem to work around it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am extremely unsure about about that. I re-read the bonus section of strategic rolls which specifies : <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">&quot;The players have roleplayed one or more scenes in which they obtained some </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">concrete advantage over the enemy </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">or harmed the enemy army (stealing its secrets, devastating its supply lines, killing a famous hero of the opposing forces, etc)&quot;</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">I truly believe that the intent of the strategic warfare roll is made to represent all these specific scenes and advantages accumulated before the fight. I think it works this way for the 2 following reasons : 1) It avoids players spending all their life recruiting stuff in order to win the war in a way that spends time escalating with the ST and the ennemies NPC's armies.</span></span></span><br /> 2) It allows to use them depending on the result of the Strategic warfare roll : When PC's win, they can explain how their hard work payed. When they lose (the roll ofc), we must find out why, while they accumulated advantage, the adversary prove having a better advantage or a counter advantage. Maybe they bought salt mines and prevented the ghosts PC's recruited to come into battle, etc ... I don't think this is supposed to be fixed, in fact it could make for interesting side objectives during the battle, but I think the advantages of the players are somewhat supposed to show off because of the strategic warfare roll, not despite of it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-27T01:09:10-08:00","id":1363575,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1363566#post1363566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I am extremely unsure about about that. I re-read the bonus section of strategic rolls which specifies : <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">&quot;The players have roleplayed one or more scenes in which they obtained some </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">concrete advantage over the enemy </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">or harmed the enemy army (stealing its secrets, devastating its supply lines, killing a famous hero of the opposing forces, etc)&quot;</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">I truly believe that the intent of the strategic warfare roll is made to represent all these specific scenes and advantages accumulated before the fight. I think it works this way for the 2 following reasons : 1) It avoids players spending all their life recruiting stuff in order to win the war in a way that spends time escalating with the ST and the ennemies NPC's armies.</span></span></span><br /> 2) It allows to use them depending on the result of the Strategic warfare roll : When PC's win, they can explain how their hard work payed. When they lose (the roll ofc), we must find out why, while they accumulated advantage, the adversary prove having a better advantage or a counter advantage. Maybe they bought salt mines and prevented the ghosts PC's recruited to come into battle, etc ... I don't think this is supposed to be fixed, in fact it could make for interesting side objectives during the battle, but I think the advantages of the players are somewhat supposed to show off because of the strategic warfare roll, not despite of it.<br /> <br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I have to disagree. <br /> <br /> RE 1: Players don't (or at least shouldn't) have the luxury of unlimited time. If they do, why aren't they just ignoring the enemy army that's determined to do nothing for the rest of forever? Self-solving problem right there. <br /> If the players don't use their army to solve the problem that needs an army to solve when the time comes, then that army-sized problem is doing <i>whatever the hell it pleases</i>. <br /> <br /> RE 2: Three dice is nothing. I can get almost that much benefit by remembering &quot;Oh yeah, big roll I should blow a WP&quot;. If the group blew a session preparing for a battle and all we got for that time was <i>three measly dice</i> I'd be starting an argument right then and there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25764-vwllss-trnt-prncss","name":"vwllss trnt prncss","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"244"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-27T01:20:37-08:00","id":1363576,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vwllss trnt prncss</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1363575#post1363575\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">RE 2: Three dice is nothing. I can get almost that much benefit by remembering &quot;Oh yeah, big roll I should blow a WP&quot;. If the group blew a session preparing for a battle and all we got for that time was <i>three measly dice</i> I'd be starting an argument right then and there.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not saying that's what I want, I'm saying I think that's what the system is written for. Else, I'm not sure where the &quot;players have role-played one or more scenes in which they obtained some concrete advantage over the enemy&quot; should apply.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-27T07:27:15-08:00","id":1363623,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1363576#post1363576\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not saying that's what I want, I'm saying I think that's what the system is written for. Else, I'm not sure where the &amp;quot;players have role-played one or more scenes in which they obtained some concrete advantage over the enemy&amp;quot; should apply.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well obtaining secret battle plans or disrupting supply lines or killing one of their champions doesn\u2019t necessarily correlate directly to a specific advantage in the combat. Stealing their plans might affect how easily you can plot against their strategies without actually giving your guys a buff that combat.<br /> <br /> Some also could be pulling double duty. If you kill a champion of the enemy army then you get a +1 to your strategy roll, but even if you fail that champion is dead. It\u2019s not like you kill him but then fail the roll so your advantage goes away and he gets brought back to life to fight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-27T09:01:45-08:00","id":1363633,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1363623#post1363623\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well obtaining secret battle plans or disrupting supply lines or killing one of their champions doesn\u2019t necessarily correlate directly to a specific advantage in the combat. Stealing their plans might affect how easily you can plot against their strategies without actually giving your guys a buff that combat.<br /> <br /> Some also could be pulling double duty. If you kill a champion of the enemy army then you get a +1 to your strategy roll, but even if you fail that champion is dead. It\u2019s not like you kill him but then fail the roll so your advantage goes away and he gets brought back to life to fight.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with you on the descriptive level, but I feel something is off. I'll try to make an example to clarify what I think of it.<br /> <br /> Suppose there is a faction of people that oppose the enemy armies that are coming for you, but your players never interacted with them. Surely, these guys would participate in a fight against the invader, but you wouldn't give your PC a bonus for the strategic warfare roll, because the advantage technically exists but it doesn't relate to your story (because the PC are not interacting with them). Now suppose your PC actually interacted with them, and successfully helped them join the cause, now you would give your players the strategic warfare bonus, because it feels good for them to have the bonus associated with something they spent time and energy for. But technically, this faction is the same as before, and will fight against the opposing armies in the same way than before. You could also add them as another battle group, but I think most of the time it will 1) Make the fight much more technical because you have more units to manage, 2) Make the fight much more unfair to the ennemies, because we all know the strength of divided battle groups.<br /> <br /> I would also like to add that, since we are playing Exalted, this situation is rather common, and usually with even more factions involved : I've seen situations where the player assemble the elementals nearby, convince the shadowy faction of the town, and successfully plead the beast-god and his beastly armies to come to help. I think creating Battle Groups and stuff for everyone of them goes contrary to the idea of &quot;Battle groups simplifying the battle&quot; because you have too much of them, and on top of that we add the unfairness mentioned that the power of all these usually &quot;little&quot; allies make when incarnated as Battle Groups.<br /> <br /> I think there are tools to make the battle more interesting or awarding for the players (the elemental give might to the soldiers because their weapon are imbued with elemental power, the terrain might be difficult terrain because it is swarming what dangerous beasts, warfare strategy bonus on the roll, etc ...) but I think straight taking the system and creating Battle Groups is counter productive, and usually unfair to the ST side as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-27T09:43:32-08:00","id":1363645,"title":"","content":"Yeah if you\u2019re getting like three or four different allied factions involved it would probably be better not to involve them in the combat scene, if they\u2019re not rolled in with the other battlegroups they could be off somewhere \u201coffscreen\u201d dealing with other things, but still giving a bonus to the strategic roll. <br /> <br /> It might depend what they are though, if you secure an ally in the form of a battlegroup of Might 2 elementals or a Solar exalt or something you might consider adding them to the battle. Though at that point you might only want to add one or the other. <br /> <br /> If you\u2019ve already got 5 PCs, and then maybe 5 antagonists between champions and battlegroups it\u2019s enough that you don\u2019t want to add much to that if anything. On the other hand stuff like terrain or cover don\u2019t add any additional initiative tracks, but can change the battlefield a lot or add some flavour.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-27T13:33:16-08:00","id":1363672,"title":"","content":"I'd just fold them into existing battlegroups or give them unique effects. <br /> The elementals agree to fight alongside your men. A battle group of your choice with Size 2 or lower gains +1 Might, and +4 Magnitude. Alternatively, a battle group of any size gains +1 Might but no Magnitude bonus. <br /> The beastmen agree to fight alongside your men. A battle group of your choice gains +1 Might, +1 Magnitude. If it's Size 3 or lower, it gains +1 Size but -1 Drill. <br /> The shadowy people agree to aid your schemes. Before the battle starts, they make an roll using their Sneaky Bastards pool against the Senses pool of the enemy's sentries. If they succeed, all enemy battle groups take -1 Magnitude and -1 Damage due to slow acting poisons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25764-vwllss-trnt-prncss","name":"vwllss trnt prncss","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"244"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-27T23:28:52-08:00","id":1363745,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vwllss trnt prncss</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1362822-what-level-of-strategy-do-you-consider-is-good-for-fighting?p=1363672#post1363672\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd just fold them into existing battlegroups or give them unique effects. <br /> The elementals agree to fight alongside your men. A battle group of your choice with Size 2 or lower gains +1 Might, and +4 Magnitude. Alternatively, a battle group of any size gains +1 Might but no Magnitude bonus. <br /> The beastmen agree to fight alongside your men. A battle group of your choice gains +1 Might, +1 Magnitude. If it's Size 3 or lower, it gains +1 Size but -1 Drill. <br /> The shadowy people agree to aid your schemes. Before the battle starts, they make an roll using their Sneaky Bastards pool against the Senses pool of the enemy's sentries. If they succeed, all enemy battle groups take -1 Magnitude and -1 Damage due to slow acting poisons.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes ! That's exactly the kind of thing I'm trying to think about ! <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1121_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1121\" data-vbnamecard=\"1121\">DrLoveMonkey</a> yeah at the point of 5v5 I'm full already I guess","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}